<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      BUILD-TIME ONLY (#1842). This project holds the repository's OWN
      architecture rules, written as Roslyn analyzers instead of regex scans
      over the source text.

      It is a Roslyn component: the compiler loads it and runs its rules over
      every product project. Nothing here reaches a shipped binary — the
      projects that consume it reference it with
      `<OutputItemType>Analyzer</OutputItemType>` and
      `<ReferenceOutputAssembly>false</ReferenceOutputAssembly>`, so the
      assembly is handed to the compiler and never becomes a runtime
      reference of the consuming project. The NativeAOT rule (#626) governs
      shipped code, and a compiler extension is not shipped code.

      `IsPackable=false` because the rules are internal to this repository and
      are never published as a NuGet package; `IsRoslynComponentPackage` and
      `RoslynComponent` mark what the project IS, so that a packer or a reader
      does not have to guess.
    -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsRoslynComponentPackage>true</IsRoslynComponentPackage>
    <RoslynComponent>Harbor.Arch.Analyzers</RoslynComponent>
    <EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>true</EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>
    <!--
      RS1041 asks a compiler extension to target netstandard2.0, because an
      extension may be loaded by a compiler older than the one this repository
      builds with. The rule does not hold here: global.json pins SDK
      10.0.402, and the analyzer is loaded by THAT compiler and by no other,
      so the target framework that matches the SDK is the one that can load
      it. The same suppression, with the same reason, is carried by
      src/Harbor.CodeGen.

      RS2008 asks an analyzer package to carry release-tracking files, which
      exist so that a PUBLISHED analyzer can say which diagnostics a given
      release added. This analyzer is never published — it is consumed by the
      build of this repository alone — so the files would document a release
      that does not exist. The same reasoning is why
      tests/Harbor.Architecture.Tests suppresses the rule for the analyzers it
      hosts.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);RS1041;RS2008</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The compiler API. NO Version attribute: the version is the central pin
      in /Directory.Packages.props, and writing one here would state a
      SECOND answer to the same question — which is how a restore ends up with
      an exact version constraint that conflicts with the pin and fails with
      NU1608. The pin is the only place the version is written down, and this
      project asks for the same version the source generators and the
      architecture tests already ask for.

      PrivateAssets=all: the analyzer runs inside the compiler, which already
      hosts the Roslyn runtime, so nothing may flow to a consumer of this
      project.

      Proj0037 (#1857) wants ExcludeAssets=runtime on every PrivateAssets=all
      reference. It does not hold here, and the finding is scoped at this
      element rather than silenced globally: the analyzer is compiled against
      Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp, and the local copy of the Roslyn runtime
      is what lets it load against the version it was compiled against.
      Excluding it would change how the analyzer is loaded, which is a
      separate decision with its own verification. src/Harbor.CodeGen takes
      the same position for the same reason.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" PrivateAssets="all" NoWarn="Proj0037"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The architecture tests drive these analyzers in-process (they build a
      compilation and ask the analyzer for its verdict), and one of the
      assertions they make is about the ownership TABLE, which is internal:
      a rule that owns an exception table is not more honest for publishing
      it. The same pattern is used by src/Harbor.Tools.Builtin for its own
      test project.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Harbor.Architecture.Tests"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
